# Sprint Planning — Executing
#
# The agile half of the hybrid framework. Not a PMBOK process: iteration
# planning comes from Scrum and from the Agile Practice Guide published with
# the PMBOK Guide 6th edition.

screen: sprints
title: Sprint Planning
phase: Executing
process: Agile Practice Guide — iteration planning
order: 68
requires: scope
teaches: |
  How an iteration is committed to: what capacity is, where it comes from, and
  why pulling work is different from being assigned it. By the end you should
  be able to explain what velocity measures and why it is useless as a target.
before: |
  Work packages, so the backlog holds the project's actual work rather than
  whatever came up this week. And a sense of how long an iteration is, which
  is the cadence field below.
after: |
  The board. Everything pulled into a sprint here is what the board shows in
  flight, and what the review and retrospective screen asks about afterwards.
intro: |
  Planning a sprint is choosing what to commit to, out of a backlog, against a
  capacity. Three numbers make it honest: what a card is estimated at, what the
  team can actually finish, and what it finished last time.

  Pull, do not push. Work is taken when there is room for it, not handed out to
  fill a quota. The difference sounds like manners and is actually the whole
  mechanism: a team that pulls stops when it is full, and a team that is pushed
  cannot.
source: |
  Agile Practice Guide, published with the PMBOK Guide 6th ed.: iteration
  planning, backlog refinement and velocity. The work itself is 4.3 Direct and
  Manage Project Work.

field cadence
  prompt: How long is a sprint here, and why that long?
  check words>=8: Say the length and the reason. A cadence nobody chose is a cadence nobody keeps.
  label: Cadence
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    Agile Practice Guide: iteration length, fixed for the life of the project
    so that velocity from one sprint can be compared with the next.
  why: |
    A fixed length is what makes velocity mean anything: twelve points in two
    weeks and twelve points in three are not the same measurement. Short
    iterations give you feedback sooner and cost more overhead per iteration;
    long ones are cheaper to run and hide problems for longer.
  if_blank: |
    Sprints end when the work happens to finish, which means they are not
    sprints and nothing can be measured against anything.
  example: |
    One week. Short enough that a wrong turn costs a week, and this project has
    a month in total.

field definition_of_ready
  prompt: What has to be true about a card before it can be pulled into a sprint?
  check filled: Without this, refinement never happens and every sprint starts by working out what the work is.
  label: Definition of ready
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    Agile practice: the definition of ready, the counterpart to the definition
    of done on the board.
  why: |
    It is the gate on the way in, the way the definition of done is the gate on
    the way out. A card that is not ready will spend the sprint being
    understood rather than being built, and that time comes out of the
    commitment you already made.

    Keep it short. A long definition of ready turns into a second planning
    process that has to be done before planning.
  if_blank: |
    Unrefined work gets pulled in because it is the most urgent-sounding thing
    in the backlog, and the sprint quietly becomes a research project.
  example: |
    Small enough to finish in the sprint, estimated, and with a done-when line
    somebody other than the author could check.

field sprints
  prompt: What are the iterations, and what is each one committed to?
  check rows>=1: A planning screen with no sprint on it has nothing to plan into.
  check column:name: Every sprint needs a name, because the cards refer to it by name.
  check column:capacity: A sprint with no capacity cannot be over-committed, which means the number is not doing its job.
  label: Sprints
  type: table
  origin: yours
  view: planning
  reads: board.cards
  title_column: title
  source: |
    Not a PMBOK artifact. The iteration list from agile practice, with the
    capacity number that iteration planning is done against.
  why: |
    Drag a card from the backlog into the sprint and the bar shows what you
    have committed against what you said you could finish. Going over is not
    blocked, it is shown: deciding what to drop is the planning, and the tool's
    job is to make sure the decision is made now rather than on the last day.

    Capacity is a measurement, not an ambition. It comes from the velocity
    strip at the bottom, which is what previous sprints actually finished
    against what they planned. Setting capacity above every past result is how
    a team commits to a number it has never once hit.

    Velocity is a planning input and nothing else. The moment it becomes a
    target, cards get estimated higher and the number stops measuring anything.
  if_blank: |
    Work is picked up in whatever order it is noticed, nobody can say what the
    iteration was for, and "how much can we do" stays a feeling.
  example: |
    Sprint 2 | Planning screens usable end to end | 2026-09-16 | 2026-09-22 | 18 | Active
  column ref
    label: ID
    type: line
    width: 1
  column name
    label: Sprint
    type: line
    width: 2
  column goal
    label: Sprint goal
    type: text
    width: 5
  column start
    label: Start
    type: date
    width: 2
  column end
    label: End
    type: date
    width: 2
  column capacity
    label: Capacity (points)
    type: number
    width: 1
  column status
    label: Status
    type: choice
    options: Planned=faint, Active=accent, Done=ok, Cancelled=danger
    width: 2
